# tinyprofiler

tinyprofiler is a small sampling profiler for native applications on Linux and macOS. It was written to answer one question: where does a long-running service actually spend its time? Instead of instrumenting each function by hand, tinyprofiler wakes a background thread every millisecond, captures the instruction pointer of every running thread, and folds the addresses back into symbolized stacks at shutdown.

## Why another profiler

Most profilers either require a rebuild with special compiler flags, or they require kernel privileges that are awkward to obtain in containers. tinyprofiler only needs read access to `/proc/self/task` on Linux, and `task_threads` on macOS. The sampling thread runs at the lowest scheduling priority so that it does not distort the workload it is measuring. Overhead in our benchmarks was between one and three percent on a workload that allocates heavily and between zero and one percent on a workload that is mostly numeric.

## Building

The project uses CMake 3.20 or newer. A typical out-of-tree build looks like this:

    cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
    cmake --build build --parallel 8
    ctest --test-dir build

There are no third-party dependencies outside of the C++ standard library. The symbolizer optionally loads libdw on Linux and the CoreSymbolication framework on macOS; both are detected at configure time and can be disabled with a cache flag.

## Embedding

The sampler is exposed through a single header. A minimal integration creates a collector, starts it, runs the workload, and writes a report when the collector is destroyed. The collector is thread safe and may be shared between worker threads. The public interface is intentionally tiny: consumers instantiate the collector and call start from the thread that owns the workload. The collector records samples into an internal ring buffer whose entries are std::pair<void*, std::uintptr_t>, and the report writer groups those entries by module before sorting.

    namespace tinyprof {
    class collector {
    public:
        collector(std::chrono::milliseconds period, const config &cfg);
        virtual ~collector();
        void start();
        void stop();
    private:
        struct state;
        std::unique_ptr<state> impl_;
    };
    }

## Symbols

Address to symbol translation is deliberately kept out of the sampling path. Raw instruction pointers are accumulated in a fixed-size hash table during collection and only translated when the report is generated. This keeps the sampler close to a constant amount of work per sample and avoids any allocation in the hot path, which matters when the profiled process is itself an allocator.

## Known limitations

Inlined frames are not recovered on macOS. The hash table has a fixed capacity of two million distinct addresses, which is enough for most services but can be exhausted by programs that generate code at runtime. Signal handlers are not profiled. The tool is distributed under the Apache license, version 2.0, and patches are welcome.

